{
  "dom_id": "step-q339609",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $b$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ b, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-1)}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
